  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nHigh pressure over the region will start to shift east tonight \nas ridge axis currently over the Northern Rockies starts to move\nslowly into the Plains. Most of the model solutions have a weak\nshortwave coming through the top of the ridge into the Plains\nlate Friday. A few of of the models have a narrow band of 2000 \nJ/kg of CAPE and 40 kts of deep layer bulk shear nosing into our\nfar southwestern counties Friday evening. On the other hand, \nHREF updraft helicities are fairly unimpressive, and NBM box and\nwhisker plots for southeastern ND range from nothing up to 1500\nJ/kg. The machine learning has some low chances for severe\nbrushing our southwestern corner Friday, but have higher \nprobabilities for Saturday and Sunday when flow aloft turns \nmore zonal to southwesterly. Instability is a little more\nuncertain over the weekend depending on where a frontal boundary\nends up, with most solutions having the best CAPE values well to\nthe southwest when the good forcing arrives late Sunday. All\nthis to say, there are some chances for severe over the weekend,\nbut not high enough to start messaging specifics at this point.\n\n\nNear zonal to southwesterly flow aloft continues into next week.\nTemps look to be above average with several chances for\nprecipitation as weak shortwaves come through. Timing and\nintensity of any storms still uncertain with such weak forcing\nand unknown instability.",
